fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1055", description: some(value: "Bootstrap must REFUSE a declaration it cannot emit correctly, instead of emitting uncompilable Scala -- measured, 27 of 44 prelude files do not compile, and every one of them emitted quietly.\n\nDECIDED 2026-08-08 (rssh): keep Bootstrap minimal -- it stays parse-IR-driven with no KB and no typer (proposal 034) -- and make the constructions it cannot handle a LOAD-TIME REFUSAL rather than a plausible-looking emission. This is the WI-891 inversion applied to scala-gen: cpp-gen turned an unlowerable body from a silent zero into a build failure. `BootstrapError` already exists and already does exactly this for one case (the eponymous-variant-alongside-siblings shape, WI-940), so the mechanism is in place and this ticket widens its remit.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS FOR WI-1020: that ticket requires the emitted prelude CLOSURE to compile, and says \"any file excluded from compilation must be named in the test with its reason -- no silent skips\". A refusal IS that named exclusion. Without this the closure test cannot be written at all, because the files that would fail are precisely the ones nothing refuses.\n\nMEASURED INVENTORY (WI-1020's harness, commit 0ebec357), split by whether the information Bootstrap needs is ALREADY IN THE PARSE IR. This split is the ticket's main content -- the first group is a fix, the second is a refusal.\n\n== GROUP A: information IS present and is dropped. FIX, do not refuse. ==\n\nA1. Operation type parameters are never read. `Operation.typeParams: IndexedSeq[TypeParam]` exists (parse/IR.scala:373); grep for it in OpGen.scala and Bootstrap.scala returns NOTHING. So `operation pure[A](a: A) -> M[A]` emits `def pure(a: A): M[A]` with `A` unbound. Affects monad, delay, and every operation with its own type params.\n\nA2. A sort's HEAD type parameters are not collected. `renderMainSort`'s `typeParams` takes only `Item.AbstractSortItem`, so `sort anthill.prelude.Monad[M[T]]` emits `trait Monad:` with no parameters at all. (`SortWithBody.isTypeParam`, IR.scala:339-345, is how the parser marks these; the loader reads it and Bootstrap does not.)\n\nA3. A sort's OWN name in a member signature loses its type arguments. `sort Cell[V]` emits `trait Cell[V]:` and then `def new(v: V): Cell`. The enclosing sort's parameters are in hand at `renderMainSort`; `TypeGen.render` is simply never told them. Same mechanism as WI-1021's second half -- see the feedback on that ticket; fix once, in the same place.\n\n== GROUP B: information is genuinely absent without a KB. REFUSE. ==\n\nB1. A name that resolves to nothing Bootstrap can see. `Term`, `NodeOccurrence`, `Type`, `Duration` reach the output as bare identifiers that name nothing in the emitted tree (effects, meta, sort). Bootstrap cannot know whether such a name is a sort in another file, an import, or a typo -- that is a resolution question.\n\nB2. `TypeExpr.Variable` renders as `?`, which is not a Scala type (TypeGen.scala:18-21); `Denoted` and `EffectRow` render as `Any` placeholders (:29-39). WI-1020's own text flags these as the open question of what to do about files that legitimately cannot compile. The answer is now: refuse them.\n\nB3. Prelude type-map arity mismatches: anthill `Stream[Element, E]` maps to Scala `LazyList`, which takes ONE parameter, so `Iterable` emits `def iterator(c: C): LazyList[Element, E]` -- `Too many type arguments`. Whether the map entry is wrong or the mapping is unrepresentable is WI-1021's question (it already asks about `List`/`Set`/`Map`/`Stream`); until that is settled a refusal beats an emission.\n\nSCOPE: fix Group A; refuse Group B through `BootstrapError`, naming the declaration and WHY it cannot be emitted. Then WI-1020's closure test becomes writable: generate the prelude, compile what was emitted, and assert the refusal set equals a named, explained list.\n\nA REFUSAL IS NOT A SKIP -- it must name the declaration, its span, and the construct that defeated it, the way the existing `BootstrapError` does. A caller catching it and continuing would recreate exactly the blindness this closes.\n\nWATCH OUT: refusing too much makes the backend useless -- 16 of 44 prelude files compile TODAY, and that number must not go DOWN. State it as a floor in the test.\n\nACCEPTANCE: Group A fixed, driven by the compile harness (monad/cell emissions compile). Group B refused with a located message per case, each pinned by a test asserting the message names the declaration. The count of prelude files that compile is asserted and is >= 16. Say at each test site which assertion fails when that arm is backed out. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1020's prelude sweep."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T14:44:23Z"))
